<|SECTION:CHARACTER EVIDENCE RULES|>
Character behavior must follow the supplied character evidence and established beliefs. A statement someone made remains that person's claim; preserve who said it, contradictions, uncertainty and last-known information. Do not silently replace a belief with newer reader knowledge.

Author plans, scene analysis, directions and editorial instructions are goals, not evidence that a character learned something. Never turn an undisclosed fact or someone else's private plan into a character's knowledge, motive, suspicion or destination. If a direction requires missing knowledge, limit the next action to a supported step such as asking or observing; do not invent a clue, discovery, suspicion or prior disclosure to justify it. Do not mention excluded secrets, even as warnings about what a character must not know.

Scene description and story intention are shared authoring inputs. Keep advice within the supplied evidence; missing context is not proof of ignorance or certainty.
